OpenWings patch 0025: Connect your accounts (the free connections)

Mail, calendars and files in one place, each person connecting their own,
with nothing to pay and nothing for the project owner to run. The flows,
storage and rules live in OpenWings' own modules (wings/accounts.py, the
shared plumbing patch 0026 reuses for Microsoft; wings/accounts_mail.py,
wings/ics_feeds.py, wings/accounts_google.py, wings/accounts_routes.py);
this patch wires them into upstream:

  * static/js/wings-connect.js: "Connect your apps" in Settings >
    Integrations becomes "Connect your accounts", grouped as Mail and
    calendar (Gmail, Google Calendar and Drive, iCloud, Yahoo, Calendar by
    link; the card list lets patch 0026 add Outlook / Microsoft 365 after
    Yahoo), Files and notes (Notion) and Work apps (Linear, Jira and
    Confluence), plus one line on what is not possible (Google Keep, Apple
    Notes; Zoom later). Each card shows this person's state (Connected as,
    Connect, Disconnect, Refresh now); members see a work app as connected
    only when the admin's connection is up.
    Dialogs: app password with numbered steps and the providers' own pages,
    Calendar by link (read only, a secret link works like a password),
    Sign in with Google with ticks for Mail, Calendar and Drive, and the
    admin's one-time Google key wizard (console links, the exact redirect
    URI with a copy button and Google's rules for it, Publish the app).
    Dialogs close with Escape before the window behind them, keep Tab
    inside and give focus back. Coming back from Google, the card opens
    and a note says what was connected, or why not.
  * static/js/settings.js, static/js/calendar.js: the Email "add account"
    forms and the Calendar's empty state and settings point to those cards;
    upstream's forms stay as "Other / manual". Events from a calendar added
    by link open read only (no Edit, Delete or dragging; the server's reason
    is shown); "Add a Google Meet link" on Google calendars.
  * routes/email/email_routes.py: upstream's "Connect with Google" and its
    callback run through wings/accounts_google.py: one callback for every
    Google sign-in, a state bound to the person who started it, used once
    within ten minutes, S256 PKCE, and the Google client an admin saved
    (encrypted) or the .env GOOGLE_OAUTH_* values. Upstream's checks stay:
    owner, a fresh refresh token, the same mailbox identity.
  * app.py: a provider callback that arrives without a session on another
    trusted address than the one the sign-in started from (localhost vs
    127.0.0.1) is sent back there, same path and query, from the address
    stored with the attempt; start-up adds the per-person assistant tools
    and the 30 minute refresh of calendars added by link.
  * routes/email/email_helpers.py, mcp_servers/email_server.py: IMAP and
    SMTP sign in with XOAUTH2 for any OAuth mail account (wings.accounts.
    mail_token), so the assistant's mail tools work with Google accounts
    too, and a token is only ever sent to that provider's own mail servers;
    Google token refresh uses the saved client.
  * src/caldav_sync.py, src/caldav_writeback.py: a CalDAV account signed in
    with Google uses an OAuth bearer token against Google's CalDAV endpoint
    (two way, through upstream's own sync and write-back).
  * routes/calendar_routes.py, src/tools/calendar.py, src/tool_schemas.py:
    calendars added by link are read only for the routes and the
    assistant and never become the default calendar; calendars list
    read_only and google; manage_calendar create_event takes google_meet
    and marks link calendar text as outside text for the safety gate;
    deleting a link calendar forgets the link.
  * routes/chat_routes.py, src/tool_capabilities.py: the Google Drive tools
    are offered only to a person who connected Drive, and the safety gate
    knows them from import (read private, outside text).
  * tests/test_email_oauth.py: the callback tests start a real sign-in
    flow (state, PKCE, person) instead of a bare signed state.

